"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][19]={"university_physics_en:M19-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M19-L1 – Measurement Design, Blank Values and Uncertainty Budgets<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: uncertainty; M18: experimental\nplanning and counting statistics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Build an uncertainty budget for\na difference measurement, account for correlation and identify its\nlimits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>A demanding measurement begins with a measurement model rather than\nan instrument. A sample reading often requires subtracting a blank:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=s-b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth the signal estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the background estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequire units, acquisition duration, uncertainty information and a\ntraceable record. A blank is an experimental control; blinded analysis\ninstead hides group identity until the analysis is frozen. These two\nmeanings must not be conflated. Temperature drift, offset errors and\ncommon calibration can introduce correlations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>For standard uncertainties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlinear propagation gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cov<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_y^2=u_s^2+u_b^2-2\\,\\operatorname{cov}(s,b).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nIf the readings are independent, the covariance vanishes. Unknown\ndependence requires a documented assumption rather than silently\ntreating the terms as independent. Repeats inform random variability,\nbut they cannot exclude calibration bias. Report a result as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\pm u_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntogether with the uncertainty definition and units; extra decimal digits\nare not evidence of accuracy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>In an illustrative experiment,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_s=180<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_b=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts are observed in equal time intervals. Independent Poisson counts\nyield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>140<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=140<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnet events with estimated standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>180<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.83<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_n=\\sqrt{180+40}\\approx14.83<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevents. The net-count signal-to-noise ratio is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>9.44<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.44<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf blank acquisition lasted twice as long, its count must first be\nscaled to the sample interval and its variance multiplied by the square\nof that scaling factor. The diagram distinguishes total signal, blank\nand net estimate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_013_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A schematic count plot shows 180 total, 40 blank and 140 net events.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A schematic count plot shows 180 total,\n40 blank and 140 net events.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A schematic count plot shows 180 total, 40 blank and 140 net\nevents.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does covariance represent when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=s-b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the net count and standard uncertainty for independent totals\n180 and 40.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can ten repeated readings not eliminate a constant calibration\nbias?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It captures shared variation between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\npositive covariance reduces the variance of their difference.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>140<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=140<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>220<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.83<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_n=\\sqrt{220}\\approx14.83<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevents.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The fixed offset remains in every repeat; an independent calibration\ncheck is required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>The Poisson assumption requires effectively independent arrivals and\na sufficiently stable rate during acquisition. Dead time, drift or\nclustered arrivals require a different model. State exclusion rules,\nrepeats and calibration controls before inspecting the result. A highly\nprecise estimate can still be biased. The illustration is a teaching\ndiagram with synthetic counts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A defensible measurement model connects corrections, covariance,\ncontrols and declared assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Vacuum Technology, Residual Gas\nand Mean Free Path<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/nist-technical-note-1297/nist-tn-1297-appendix-law-propagation-uncertainty\">NIST – TN 1297: Appendix A. Law of Propagation of Uncertainty<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A.3–A.4, equations A-3 to A-5; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L1 – Measurement Design, Blank Values and Uncertainty Budgets · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dfg.de/en/basics-topics/digital-topics/research-data/recommendations\">DFG – Subject-specific Recommendations on the Handling of Research Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Natural sciences, lines 629–645; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L1 – Measurement Design, Blank Values and Uncertainty Budgets · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L1 – Measurement Design, Blank Values and Uncertainty Budgets · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M19-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M19-L2 – Vacuum Technology, Residual Gas and Mean Free Path<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: kinetic gas theory; M16:\nmetrology; M18: safety. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model vacuum as a finite\npressure regime, estimate a mean free path and identify measurement\nartefacts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>A laboratory vacuum is a reduced gas density, not literally empty\nspace. Chamber pressure depends on temperature, pumping speed,\nconductance, leaks and gas desorption from surfaces. A residual-gas\nspectrum can help diagnose a system, but its intensities do not directly\nprove species concentrations without calibration. Pressure gauges rely\non different operating principles; their readings are not\ninterchangeable outside their stated ranges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>The ideal kinetic hard-sphere model predicts a mean free path\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=\\frac{k_{\\mathrm B}T}{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi d^2p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis absolute temperature,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nan effective collision diameter and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npressure. Holding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\propto p^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComparing the path with chamber dimensions distinguishes flow regimes.\nThis simplified relation does not automatically cover gas mixtures or\nstrongly non-thermal conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.37<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.37\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.380649<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe mean free path is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.81\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTen times the pressure decreases it to about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.681<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.681\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth values can be compared with a chamber dimension of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto assess the flow regime. The graphic shows the inverse relation on\nlogarithmic scales; its points are model predictions, not\nobservations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_308_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Model curve for inverse pressure dependence at fixed temperature and molecular size.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model curve for inverse pressure\ndependence at fixed temperature and molecular size.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Model curve for inverse pressure dependence at fixed temperature\nand molecular size.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing the example.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a residual-gas peak not unambiguous evidence of one chemical\nspecies?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.681<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.681\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause the pressure is tenfold.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spectra may contain overlapping fragments, and sensitivity and\ncalibration must be considered.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Real systems can be dominated by water vapour, oils, temperature\nvariation, gauge sensitivity and surface outgassing. A mass-spectrum\npeak need not identify a unique molecule without further evidence and\ncalibration. Glass under differential pressure, high voltage and\ncryogenic equipment require authorised laboratory procedures. This is a\nmodel lesson, not operating instructions for an unprotected system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Pressure, temperature and collision model set the mean free path;\nspectra need separate calibration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Precision Spectroscopy and\nZeeman Splitting<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.uni-potsdam.de/en/ppf/versuche/mono-bachelor/m4-raman-spektroskopie-1\">Universität Potsdam – M9 Vakuum und Massenspektroskopie<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 356–361; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L2 – Vacuum Technology, Residual Gas and Mean Free Path · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L2 – Vacuum Technology, Residual Gas and Mean Free Path · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M19-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M19-L3 – Precision Spectroscopy and Zeeman Splitting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M11: spin and spectral lines; M15:\ndetectors; M18: spectroscopy. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a simple Zeeman\nsplitting and distinguish calibration from line identification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Spectroscopy compares optical frequency or wavelength with\ntransitions in atoms or molecules. Observed line positions also depend\non instrumental response, calibration, temperature, magnetic field and\nresolution. An applied magnetic field splits previously degenerate\nmagnetic sublevels. For suitable states in weak fields, the linear\nZeeman approximation applies, but an observed line is always a\ndifference between two energy levels subject to selection rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>For one level with Landé factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe linear approximation is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{m_J}=g_J\\,\\mu_{\\mathrm B}\\,m_J\\,B.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThe frequency difference between adjacent sublevels of that same term is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=g_J\\mu_{\\mathrm B}B/h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a level separation, not automatically the splitting of a\nmeasured optical line: upper/lower level factors and allowed transitions\nmatter. Strong fields, hyperfine interactions and altered\nangular-momentum coupling call for other models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.10\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_J=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>13.996<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm B}/h\\approx13.996\\,\\mathrm{GHz/T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdjacent model sublevels are then separated by approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.799<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.799\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin frequency. In this linear approximation the separation vanishes at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure uses an illustrative triplet with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_J=-1,0,+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit is not a spectrum of a specific atom and makes no claim about optical\ntransition strengths.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_323_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three illustrative energy levels split linearly as the field increases.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three illustrative energy levels split\nlinearly as the field increases.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Three illustrative energy levels split linearly as the field\nincreases.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which term changes with magnetic field in the linear Zeeman\nmodel?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_J=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.10\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a level separation not directly equal an optical line\nsplitting?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The level shift is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_Jm_JB<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.799<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu\\approx2.799\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An optical transition connects two levels with selection rules, and\nboth levels may shift differently.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Real analysis requires wavelength calibration, the instrumental\nline-spread function, field homogeneity, polarisation selection and a\ncheck for overlapping features. One observed peak does not establish a\nZeeman effect. Evidence improves when peak positions and polarisation\nchange with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas predicted. Optical sources and magnetic fields may carry significant\nhazards and require authorised local operating procedures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Magnetic splitting is meaningful only together with a level model,\ntransitions and instrument calibration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Weighted Regression and Model\nCriticism<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L3 – Precision Spectroscopy and Zeeman Splitting · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L3 – Precision Spectroscopy and Zeeman Splitting · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M19-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M19-L4 – Weighted Regression and Model Criticism<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: regression; M17: computation;\nM18: measurement reports. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate a weighted fit with\nresiduals and degrees of freedom, and assess model assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Regression does not automatically validate a physical model. It\nestimates parameters inside a chosen function family under a stipulated\nerror model. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i=a+bx_i+\\epsilon_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nspecify the units and whether uncertainty in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be neglected. Calibration reference values can induce correlations.\nMaximising\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not replace a residual study or a physical explanation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>With uncorrelated observations and known standard uncertainties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na common objective is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>N<\/mi><\/munderover><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2(a,b)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{[y_i-(a+bx_i)]^2}{u_i^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nA two-parameter fit to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent points ordinarily has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=N-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresidual degrees of freedom. However,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2/\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a universal pass/fail score: estimated, correlated or\nnon-Gaussian errors alter the interpretation and reference distribution.\nAssumptions must be checked before applying thresholds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Use three synthetic readings\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)=(0,1.1),(1,3.0),(2,5.1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_y=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIdentical weights yield a least-squares line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.0667<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y\\approx1.0667+2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nResiduals are approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.0333<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.0667<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.0333<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(+0.0333,-0.0667,+0.0333)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.6667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2\\approx0.6667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThree points are too few to assess residual distributions reliably or\ngeneralise to arbitrary experiments. They serve as a transparent worked\nexample. The figure separates readings, fitted line and vertical\nresiduals.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_317_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three observations, their fitted line and vertical residuals.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three observations, their fitted line and\nvertical residuals.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Three observations, their fitted line and vertical\nresiduals.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many residual degrees of freedom does a two-parameter line\nfitted to three points have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0667<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=1.0667+2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2\\approx1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone not validate a physical model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=3-2=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.0667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y\\approx3.0667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An apparently excellent curve fit can conceal measurement errors,\ncorrelations or a wrong model family.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Alongside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexamine parameter units, treatment of outliers, temporal structure and\ndependence of residuals on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA curved residual pattern suggests an inadequate model class, whereas\nrandom-looking residuals do not prove physical correctness. Nonlinear or\ncorrelated problems call for appropriate covariance models and numerical\ntests. Preserve raw data and the full fitting procedure for\nreproducibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Parameters, uncertainty and residuals must be evaluated along with\nthe assumptions of the model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Research Questions, Literature\nand Blinded Analysis<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://roesner.pages.ub.uni-bielefeld.de/lectures/computational-physics/semester/sose2026/overview/03_scope.html\">Computational Physics SoSe 2026 – Scope<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter index, lines 12–73; competencies and content, lines 87–98; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L4 – Weighted Regression and Model Criticism · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L4 – Weighted Regression and Model Criticism · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M19-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M19-L5 – Research Questions, Literature and Blinded Analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18: laboratory reports; M19-L1:\nmeasurement model; basic statistics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Separate source evidence from\noriginal findings and justify a prespecified analysis plan.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Research begins with a question narrow enough to determine what data\ncould answer it. Literature review first establishes terms and methods:\nprimary papers, textbooks, lab manuals and reviews serve different\nevidential purposes. Record author, title, version, passage and\npublication date for important claims. An item merely listed in a\nbibliography does not verify every equation in a course. A sound\ninvestigation also specifies competing hypotheses and possible\nartefacts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>A prespecified analysis plan states the primary outcome, quality\nthresholds, exclusion rules and test before outcomes are seen. Blinding\ncan reduce selective interpretation induced by knowing group labels; it\ncannot substitute for instrument calibration. Hypotheses developed after\nlooking at data are acceptable as exploratory analyses when labelled\naccordingly. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent tests with false-alarm probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach, a simplified teaching model gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\ge 1}=1-(1-\\alpha)^m.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nIndependence is an assumption, not a universal property of scientific\nstudies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent null hypotheses are tested at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe chance of at least one false positive is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.95<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.642<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.95^{20}\\approx0.642<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne significant finding selected from twenty exploratory tests must not\nbe reported as if it came from one predeclared test. The figure shows a\nsimple process: question, preregistered plan, acquisition, blinded\nanalysis, unblinding and interpretation. The calculation is\nillustrative, not an empirical rate for a research discipline.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_024_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Workflow from a prespecified question to declared interpretation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Workflow from a prespecified question to\ndeclared interpretation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Workflow from a prespecified question to declared\ninterpretation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a literature hit not automatically direct verification of a\nformula?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For 20 independent tests with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhat is the chance of at least one false alarm?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do blinded and exploratory analyses differ?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Topic relevance does not replace checking the exact passage and\nassumptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>64.2<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">64.2\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassuming independence.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Blind means hidden group labels; exploratory means hypotheses\ndeveloped after inspecting data and labelled as such.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Good reporting distinguishes planned analyses, post-hoc decisions,\nnegative findings and unperformed measurements. A research-data\nrecommendation does not imply that every raw dataset may be published\nopenly; privacy, contracts and disciplinary constraints can apply.\nChanges introduced after the results become visible must be declared as\ndeviations. The diagram is an educational workflow, not a claim that all\nresearch follows one procedure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Plans, results and post-hoc interpretation must be separately\ndocumented.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Research Data, Metadata and\nReproducibility<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dfg.de/en/basics-topics/digital-topics/research-data/recommendations\">DFG – Subject-specific Recommendations on the Handling of Research Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Natural sciences, lines 629–645; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L5 – Research Questions, Literature and Blinded Analysis · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L5 – Research Questions, Literature and Blinded Analysis · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M19-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M19-L6 – Research Data, Metadata and Reproducibility<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18: laboratory notebooks; M17:\ncomputational environments; M19-L5: analysis plans. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design a traceable dataset with\nprovenance, units, checksums and access controls.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Research data are more than arrays of numbers. Reproducibility\nrequires raw measurements, instrument configuration, calibration state,\nmetadata, analysis code, parameters and traceable connections among\nthem. A table lacking units or time references may not be reusable. FAIR\nprinciples aim at findability, accessibility under defined conditions,\ninteroperability and reusability. FAIR does not mean all raw data must\nbe openly released or retained forever regardless of context.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>Each observation needs a stable raw format plus a field list\ndocumenting units, acquisition interval and origin. A manifest can\nrecord relative paths, MIME types, sizes, generating software and\nSHA-256 digests. A hash detects byte-level changes but establishes\nneither scientific accuracy nor authorship. Provenance links inputs,\ntransformations and outputs. Retain the raw original and create a\nderived version instead of silently editing measurements in place.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SHA256<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(D)=\\operatorname{SHA256}(D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>A small synthetic package could contain <code>raw/counts.csv<\/code>\n(time in seconds and counts), <code>meta/instrument.json<\/code>\n(instrument ID and calibration status), <code>analysis/fit.py<\/code>\n(software version and parameters) and <code>results/summary.csv<\/code>\n(net counts and uncertainty). A <code>manifest.json<\/code> links the\nfour files; a README identifies them as a teaching example. The figure\nillustrates their dependencies. If exclusion rules change, retain a new,\ndocumented script and result version. Local checksum verification tests\ncopy integrity, not the physical validity of the model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_243_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A manifest links raw data, metadata and analysis code to results.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A manifest links raw data, metadata and\nanalysis code to results.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A manifest links raw data, metadata and analysis code to\nresults.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name four data components to retain besides the measured\nnumbers.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a file SHA-256 digest not guarantee?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does FAIR not automatically require open access?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example units, calibration state, analysis code and\nparameters.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Neither scientific correctness nor authorship or reliable\nacquisition.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Accessibility may be governed by legitimate restrictions and legal\nlimits.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Sensitive data may require restricted access, embargo or justified\nnon-disclosure. Retention obligations depend on actual project,\ndisciplinary and institutional rules; no universal period is asserted\nhere. Check whether an independent reader could reconstruct the path\nfrom inputs to published tables. The steps in the diagram are teaching\nrecommendations, not a universal DFG checklist.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Reproducibility relies on raw data, readable metadata, preserved\nprovenance and documented processing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Computational Records,\nConvergence and Scientific Verification<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://rdm.mpdl.mpg.de/introduction/research-data-management/\">Max Planck Digital Library – Research Data Management<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Why Data Management?, lines 9–21; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L6 – Research Data, Metadata and Reproducibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dfg.de/en/basics-topics/digital-topics/research-data/recommendations\">DFG – Subject-specific Recommendations on the Handling of Research Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Natural sciences, lines 629–645; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L6 – Research Data, Metadata and Reproducibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M19-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M19-L7 – Computational Records, Convergence and Scientific Verification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M19 – Bachelor: Advanced Experimental Methods and Research Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17: numerical methods and stability;\nM19-L4: residuals; M19-L6: data provenance. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish numerical\nverification from experimental validation and test step-size\ndependence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>A scientific computational run needs a traceable version chain from\nraw data and source code to results. Verification asks whether the\nnumerical scheme solves the stated equations adequately; validation asks\nwhether those equations represent the physical system. A convergent\nsolver can solve the wrong physical model with impressive precision.\nAnalytical limits, independent implementations, experimental controls\nand sensitivity analyses therefore serve distinct purposes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>A simple verification test approximates the derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sin x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvia the central difference\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_hf(0)=\\frac{\\sin(h)-\\sin(-h)}{2h}=\\frac{\\sin h}{h}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin h=h-h^3/6+O(h^5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_hf(0)=1-h^2/6+O(h^4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHalving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshould reduce the leading error by approximately four in the asymptotic\nregime. The rule fails when other error sources such as roundoff\ndominate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.9735459<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_h\\approx0.9735459<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith absolute error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0264541<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_h\\approx0.0264541<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.9933467<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_h\\approx0.9933467<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0066533<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_h\\approx0.0066533<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir ratio is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.976<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.976<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconsistent with second-order convergence in this range. The plot\ncompares the calculated errors against the step size with a theoretical\nreference curve. These are exact mathematical test values, not observed\ninstrument errors.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_120_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Central-difference errors for sin(x) at two step sizes with a second-order reference.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Central-difference errors for sin(x) at\ntwo step sizes with a second-order reference.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Central-difference errors for sin(x) at two step sizes with a\nsecond-order reference.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the difference between verification and validation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How should the leading central-difference error change when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name three entries that a numerical run record must include.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Verification checks the equations are solved; validation checks\ntheir appropriateness for reality.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the asymptotic regime it falls to roughly one quarter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example code version, input checksum and grid spacing; add\nrandom seed for stochastic methods.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Record code version, dependencies, parameters, units, random seed\nwhen applicable, platform and data checksums. Run convergence and\nstability tests on the actual computational pathway, not only on the\nfinal published figure. Discuss numerical uncertainty separately from\nmeasurement and model uncertainty. Distribution also requires resolving\nlicences, access and retention rules. This worked numerical check is\nevidence for one approximation, not certification of an entire research\nproject.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A traceable run combines versioned inputs with explicit convergence\ntests.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Outlook:<\/strong> Subsequent specialisation modules will be\nadded in later batches.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://roesner.pages.ub.uni-bielefeld.de/lectures/computational-physics/semester/sose2026/overview/03_scope.html\">Computational Physics SoSe 2026 – Scope<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter index, lines 12–73; competencies and content, lines 87–98; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L7 – Computational Records, Convergence and Scientific Verification · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://theorie.ikp.physik.tu-darmstadt.de/tnp/index.php?doc=compphys\">TU Darmstadt – Computational Physics (Sommer 2025)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Overview and planned content, lines 16–32; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L7 – Computational Records, Convergence and Scientific Verification · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dfg.de/en/basics-topics/digital-topics/research-data/recommendations\">DFG – Subject-specific Recommendations on the Handling of Research Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Natural sciences, lines 629–645; Overview of topics and methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M19-L7 – Computational Records, Convergence and Scientific Verification · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
